WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.040 --> 00:00:07.580
We think of wars as relatively uncommon but
that couldn't be further from the truth.

00:00:07.580 --> 00:00:13.219
Since the end of the Second World War there
have been over 250 major wars worldwide.

00:00:13.219 --> 00:00:16.990
That equates to almost four wars per year.

00:00:16.990 --> 00:00:22.040
But war isn't a new problem invented by the
stresses and tensions of modern day life.

00:00:22.040 --> 00:00:29.310
Over the past 3,400 years humans have only
been at peace, entirely without war, for 268

00:00:29.310 --> 00:00:30.520
of them.

00:00:30.520 --> 00:00:37.070
To put that into perspective, humans have
only been at peace with one another for 8%

00:00:37.070 --> 00:00:38.820
of recorded history.

00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:42.280
All this fighting doesn't come without a heavy
price tag.

00:00:42.280 --> 00:00:47.790
Wars are ridiculously costly, both in cold,
hard cash and human lives.

00:00:47.790 --> 00:00:50.070
But what is that price tag?

00:00:50.070 --> 00:00:55.660
Let's find out the real cost of war once and
for all.

00:00:55.660 --> 00:01:03.579
Let's first try and work out the cost of war's
ugliest side effect, human lives.

00:01:03.579 --> 00:01:09.210
Every war has a different cost; so for the
purpose of illustration let's examine a recent,

00:01:09.210 --> 00:01:13.500
well documented war, the Iraq and Afghanistan
war on terror.

00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:20.310
A long, large scale and horrendously expensive
war that began in 2001 following the 9/11

00:01:20.310 --> 00:01:21.310
attacks.

00:01:21.310 --> 00:01:24.900
Which, to some extent, still goes on to this
day.

00:01:24.900 --> 00:01:52.939
The number of estimated deaths of the Iraq-Afghanistan
war between 2001 and 2014, are as follows:

00:01:52.939 --> 00:01:54.450
Yes that's right.

00:01:54.450 --> 00:02:00.150
In most wars the majority of casualties are
not from the armies themselves but civilians.

00:02:00.150 --> 00:02:04.070
And those numbers are only the deaths caused
directly by conflict.

00:02:04.070 --> 00:02:09.009
They don't include the thousands of other
indirect deaths caused by side effects of

00:02:09.009 --> 00:02:11.650
war such as disease and famine.

00:02:11.650 --> 00:02:17.450
The majority of civilian casualties during
the Iraq-Afghanistan war were caused by anti-personnel

00:02:17.450 --> 00:02:21.189
and anti-tank mines as well as ground attacks
from insurgents.

00:02:21.189 --> 00:02:25.430
There’s no denying that the human cost of
war is immeasurably horrific.

00:02:25.430 --> 00:02:31.099
So let’s take a look at the second facet
of wartime expense, the monetary cost.

00:02:31.099 --> 00:02:35.349
How much money do you really need to go to
war with another country?

00:02:35.349 --> 00:02:39.180
Again the answer entirely depends on the scale
and nature of the war.

00:02:39.180 --> 00:02:43.720
So let’s once again use the Iraq-Afghanistan
war as an example.

00:02:43.720 --> 00:02:44.720
Why?

00:02:44.720 --> 00:02:49.139
Because it was the most expensive war of the
past 50 years.

00:02:49.139 --> 00:02:54.269
It is estimated that by the time it’s over
the Iraq-Afghanistan war will cost a whopping

00:02:54.269 --> 00:02:56.940
$6 trillion dollars.

00:02:56.940 --> 00:02:58.409
To put that into perspective.

00:02:58.409 --> 00:03:07.489
$6 trillion is enough money to feed every
single starving person on Earth for 200 years.

00:03:07.489 --> 00:03:11.919
Just to clarify; with that money you could
feed a billion starving people every year

00:03:11.919 --> 00:03:13.249
for the next 200 years.

00:03:13.249 --> 00:03:17.439
But it’s not quite as simple as sending
all that money to Africa.

00:03:17.439 --> 00:03:22.340
The majority of the money the US used to pay
for the war was actually borrowed, mostly

00:03:22.340 --> 00:03:23.639
from China.

00:03:23.639 --> 00:03:29.519
The George Bush administration estimated it
would cost between $50-60 billion to invade

00:03:29.519 --> 00:03:31.769
Iraq and overthrow Saddam Hussein.

00:03:31.769 --> 00:03:36.209
That’s considerably less than the total
running cost as of today.

00:03:36.209 --> 00:03:38.389
So what went wrong?

00:03:38.389 --> 00:03:42.779
What were all those six trillion dollars of
taxpayer’s and borrowed money spent on?

00:03:42.779 --> 00:03:46.219
Well the thing is they haven’t actually
been spent yet.

00:03:46.219 --> 00:03:51.669
To date the US has paid $1.6 trillion towards
the wars in Iraq and Afghanistan.

00:03:51.669 --> 00:03:57.449
That includes everything from military equipment,
firearms, training, wages and hiring third

00:03:57.449 --> 00:04:00.010
party contractors, among other things.

00:04:00.010 --> 00:04:05.489
It’s estimated that the cost of keeping
a single American soldier in Iraq or Afghanistan

00:04:05.489 --> 00:04:09.250
for a year is $3.9 million.

00:04:09.250 --> 00:04:15.200
So where does the other estimated $4.4 trillion
come in?

00:04:15.200 --> 00:04:16.760
That comes later.

00:04:16.760 --> 00:04:21.540
The biggest cost of a war is not the war itself,
it’s the aftermath.

00:04:21.540 --> 00:04:25.910
Especially when you borrow most of the money
you used to pay for the war.

00:04:25.910 --> 00:04:30.640
The majority of the money used to pay for
the Iraq-Afghanistan war was borrowed and

00:04:30.640 --> 00:04:33.730
there’s a big load of interest to pay on
top of it.

00:04:33.730 --> 00:04:38.030
The interest is so great that over the next
few years the repayments will tally up to

00:04:38.030 --> 00:04:41.100
several trillions of dollars.

00:04:41.100 --> 00:04:46.580
But there’s an even bigger long-term cost,
veteran care and benefits.

00:04:46.580 --> 00:04:52.820
970,000 veterans of the Iraq and Afghanistan
wars have registered for disability claims.

00:04:52.820 --> 00:04:57.380
Over the course of the next 50 years payments
for the care and welfare of all those veterans

00:04:57.380 --> 00:05:02.420
will bring the total cost of the war up to
an estimated $6 trillion.

00:05:02.420 --> 00:05:08.890
But for every soldier who fights in a war,
for them, the war never ends.

00:05:08.890 --> 00:05:16.090
In 2012 more veterans of the Iraq and Afghanistan
wars committed suicide than were killed in

00:05:16.090 --> 00:05:17.090
action.

00:05:17.090 --> 00:05:22.410
This isn’t a new problem; more veterans
of the 1982 Falkland’s war have committed

00:05:22.410 --> 00:05:26.530
suicide since the war than died in combat.

00:05:26.530 --> 00:05:31.520
War has many evil facets and whether it’s
a medieval battle or a modern-day high tech

00:05:31.520 --> 00:05:34.830
skirmish, you can always count on two things.

00:05:34.830 --> 00:05:39.980
The effects will be catastrophic and the costs
will be outrageously high.

00:05:39.980 --> 00:05:43.230
But wars don’t have to be expensive.

00:05:43.230 --> 00:05:49.140
In 1920 the British forces defeated the Dervish
forces of Mohammed Adullah Hassan, who was

00:05:49.140 --> 00:05:53.910
more commonly referred to as “Mad Mullah”
amongst opposition forces.

00:05:53.910 --> 00:05:59.680
Britain’s resistance against the 20-year-old
Dervish forces was known as the Somaliland

00:05:59.680 --> 00:06:00.830
Campaign.

00:06:00.830 --> 00:06:06.810
It came to a climax during the fifth and final
war of the campaign in 1920 when the British

00:06:06.810 --> 00:06:12.550
Royal Air Force accompanied by Britain’s
Somaliland Camel Corps (yes that was actually

00:06:12.550 --> 00:06:18.900
a thing) defeated the Dervishes’ stronghold
in what was then known as British Somaliland.

00:06:18.900 --> 00:06:24.300
This war has gone down in history as “the
cheapest war in history”.

00:06:24.300 --> 00:06:28.140
Costing a grand total of just £77,000.

00:06:28.140 --> 00:06:31.400
Which is about £3.5 million by today’s
standards.

00:06:31.400 --> 00:06:36.270
That sounds expensive, but it’s actually
really bloody cheap.

00:06:36.270 --> 00:06:40.220
Most wars cost in the region of several billions
to several trillion.

00:06:40.220 --> 00:06:45.250
If most wars are so expensive and cause nothing
but turmoil and sorrow then why do we have

00:06:45.250 --> 00:06:46.640
them in the first place?

00:06:46.640 --> 00:06:51.470
It’s widely debated that wars don’t actually
solve any problems.

00:06:51.470 --> 00:06:56.370
After all we have only been without war for
8% of recorded history.

00:06:56.370 --> 00:07:01.820
If wars solved problems wouldn’t that mean
war would become less frequent?

00:07:01.820 --> 00:07:04.350
When in reality the opposite is happening.

00:07:04.350 --> 00:07:11.750
With 250 wars in the past 70 years it seems
that wars only create more problems and subsequently

00:07:11.750 --> 00:07:13.200
more wars.

00:07:13.200 --> 00:07:16.280
Many people believe wars are strictly for
profit.

00:07:16.280 --> 00:07:22.860
Yes wars are costly but they also put money
into many pockets, namely arms and military

00:07:22.860 --> 00:07:24.830
vehicle manufacturers.

00:07:24.830 --> 00:07:29.610
When the cost is human lives it’s saddening
to think that some people are profiting from

00:07:29.610 --> 00:07:30.610
it.

00:07:30.610 --> 00:07:32.780
It truly is a sickening thought.

00:07:32.780 --> 00:07:39.270
But whether profit is actually the main contributing
factor to starting a war is a topic of much

00:07:39.270 --> 00:07:41.630
debate and controversy.

00:07:41.630 --> 00:07:46.280
Every war is different, sometimes it’s driven
by profit, and other times it’s just the

00:07:46.280 --> 00:08:14.570
perverted fantasies and disillusions 
of 

00:08:14.570 --> 00:08:15.790
a doomed soul.

